From 6824ed70a4a25ac1890e62b640f37f84e2044dcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:19:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 14/23] [mya] Snapshot, attach to and read the threads of any
 process on Linux Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

On Linux, mya could only attach to its own process, and a "snapshot" read
that live process, so nothing it read held still. This gives Linux the
equivalent of a Darwin corpse.

- Snapshots are copies. forkCopy(lifetime) forks the calling process with
  _Fork (no atfork handlers, no glibc lock resets) into a child that only
  waits on a lifetime pipe: its memory is the process's at the moment of the
  fork, copy-on-write, and it lives until every write end of the pipe is
  closed. A Snapshot of this process makes such a copy and reads it through
  process_vm_readv; destroying the Snapshot closes the pipe and reaps the
  copy. A cooperating target makes its own copy and hands its pid and the
  pipe's write end to the analysis, Snapshot(process, copy, lifetime). A
  Snapshot of another process that made no copy still reads it live, as a
  best-effort view.
- Process::attach accepts any pid that exists; holdsLiveTask checks it still
  does. executablePathOf reads /proc/<pid>/exe.
- Thread::platformCollect lists /proc/<pid>/task: each thread's name, run
  state, user and system time, and, for a thread blocked in the kernel, its
  stack pointer from /proc/<pid>/task/<tid>/syscall, and so its stack region.
  A copy has only the forking thread, so the threads are the target's, read
  when the snapshot is made.
- Memory tells a hole from an unreadable page after a failed
  process_vm_readv, which says only EFAULT for both, by consulting the
  region list, as mach_vm_remap's error does on Darwin.

Tests:
- The out-of-process harness works on Linux: the target gets the read end of
  a lifetime pipe as its standard input, forks its copy when it reports its
  object (reportTargetObject), and reports the copy's pid; testLibJSCTools is
  a child subreaper so that copies outliving their target are reaped by it.
  analyzeInSeparateProcess and analyzeOutOfProcessThenRelease are added, and
  takeSnapshot is shared.
- The Memory suite's arenas are laid out portably and the suite runs on
  Linux; the Thread suite runs in and out of process, on both platforms.
- The Snapshot suite checks that a snapshot holds still while the target
  changes, that no atfork handler runs, that two snapshots are two copies, and
  on Linux that a copy lives as long as its snapshot and is reaped.

